+# Relational Choice in full rooted clouds
+
+25 October 2026. Connecting deduction by OpenAI Codex, prompted by Daniel Kodsi. Direct source: this note, §§1–2 (unpaginated), `misc`.
+
+## 1. Root fullness supplies Relational Choice
+
+Use the existing `modalized-atomless-cloud` group without changing its construction. At the isolated root r, take the quotient of the existing entities at each type by whole root identity. Lemma 6.7 of Andrew Bacon and Claude Opus 5.5, *Modalized Domains on Possibility Frames*, inspected draft of 23 October 2026, p. 31, realizes every relation on finite products of these quotient sets.
+
+Explicitly, give selected tuples the root-only proposition and all other tuples bottom. This relation preserves identity at the root because its membership depends only on whole root identity classes. At cloud points both values are locally bottom, so the identity/existence condition is preserved there as well. Full modalized domains contain the relation, including relations whose arguments are individuals.
+
+Given an actually serial relation U, metatheoretic ZFC Choice selects an output class for each input class. Represent its graph by the preceding construction. Its actual extension is contained in U and is total and single-valued for root identity. This is the exact all-type Relational Choice schema. Individual outputs are encoded relationally, so no forbidden e-valued operation is introduced.
+
+## 2. Consequences and attribution
+
+The source already gives necessary Rigid Comprehension and necessary BF, and a nonzero atomless cloud interval (Theorem 4.4 and Corollary 4.5, p. 21). Together with the new Relational Choice verdict this refutes the Atomicity implication, already at t. The entire root proposition algebra has an isolated atom; it is not atomless.
+
+The same fullness represents external well-orders. The recorded chain through Weak Rigid Comprehension and BF gives Modal Well-Ordering, hence necessary Relational Choice. Necessary Actuality and necessary Boolean Completeness follow from the existing necessary Rigid Comprehension chain. These are derived consequences, not additional independent model arguments.
+
+The argument applies to both Cohen and lottery clouds and, more generally, to the entire recorded family. It uses the source construction and fullness theorem; the new contribution is their connecting Choice deduction and resulting separation.
